## ===========================================================================
## THE EAVESDROP — Book 1's scene, carried whole
##
## The bite in the Alley is the source's opening moment (`story.ni:1469`):
## "Suddenly, from the alley's entrance, you hear voices." From there the
## 2009 game boxed Jack into the Alley and the storehouse roof above it until
## she had heard the mercenaries' whole briefing — the `Eavesdropping on
## Soldiers` scene (`story.ni:1742-1804`): men at the alley mouth whose words
## she cannot make out from below, the crates up to the Outer Market Roof,
## five turns of the leader's orders overheard from there (the parchment's
## "hat and a gray cloak" among them), and then the men fanning out into the
## market with two of them shoving the north gates closed.
##
## WHAT THIS FILE FIXES about the state model, and the reading it takes.
## Before it, the bite moved the story straight to `hunted` and started the
## sweep's arrival clock, so Jack was hunted before she had heard why. The
## change document ("The eavesdrop's aftermath") defines `hunted` as "Jack
## has heard the voices and knows she is wanted", and the gates ruling puts
## the lock "at the eavesdrop's end — the calm → hunted transition". So:
##
##   * the bite stays `calm` and opens the scene: the voices arrive at the
##     alley mouth, the group and its captain appear on the roof;
##   * the fifth overheard line ends it: the men leave, the story turns
##     `hunted`, and the north gates (`north is blocked while hunted`) are
##     locked;
##   * the sweep opens as the source opens it (`story.ni:1725`): the first
##     climb back down after the briefing is the tumble — "Check the alley!"
##     — and the pair rushes in `approaching` with their lunge clock
##     running. Moving out of the alley shakes them off (the dash-away line)
##     and restarts `waiting`; the random sweep runs from there.
##
## RULED (David, 2026-08-30): the transition is at the briefing's end. The
## change document's older sentence "the bite in the Alley moves calm →
## hunted" is superseded by a dated note in its "eavesdrop's aftermath"
## section; the bite card pins `calm`, the "Okay, go!" card pins `hunted`.
##
## THE FIVE LINES SPOOL ONLY FROM THE ROOF, as in the source: the every-turn
## clause is gated on the group being here, and `first time` … `fifth time`
## count that clause's own firings. Down in the alley the men are just
## talking, one chance in four per turn, and southeast is refused until they
## leave — so the roof is the only way through, which is what the source's
## `Instead of listening` line in the alley is hinting at.
##
## WHAT IS NOT CARRIED, with reasons:
##   * The ankle grab (`story.ni:1563`) carries its line as a blocked exit
##     (`up is blocked while the wandering mercenaries is here`, on the
##     Alley) but not its mutation — a blocked line cannot change a posture.
##     The pair arrives `approaching` with `lunge` running, so the grab
##     follows on the next turn anyway; one turn slower than the source.
##   * "Carefully, you descend the stack of crates." (`story.ni:1732`) — an
##     `entering` clause cannot tell a descent from a walk in from the
##     junction, so later descents print the room alone.
##   * The source's `[one of]` word alternations inside the alley's "still
##     talking" texture (`story.ni:1772-1781`) become whole-sentence
##     variants — deviation 6 in the story header's list.
##   * The arms and armor as their own examinable (`story.ni:1682`) — folded
##     into the group's description, which already names the mismatched
##     arms. The captain IS carried, as the scenery he is on the roof.
##   * "You can't do anything to the gates from up here" — cross-room scope
##     is not a Chord thing; the gates are examinable at the junction only.
##   * "No time for breakfast right now!" (`story.ni:2417`) — the engine
##     keeps the bitten apple at zero servings and stdlib refuses a second
##     bite in `validate` ("There's nothing left of the apple to eat"),
##     before any story clause is consulted. Gentry's line has no way in.
##
## Every line below is Gentry's, carried from `story.ni:1474-1804`.
## ===========================================================================

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE VOICES — the men at the alley mouth, heard but not seen
##
## The source's `conversation` backdrop as it stands in the Alley. Off-stage
## until the bite moves it in, and off-stage again when the briefing ends —
## `move … offstage`, not `remove`: a removed entity cannot be named by the
## alley's `southeast is blocked while the voices is here` (entering threw,
## then the exit stopped parsing — GH #330), and a phrase emitted in the
## same arm as its owner's departure never rendered (GH #329). Off-stage
## keeps the names resolvable; the room-owned spool keeps the text.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the voices
  aka voice, men, group of men, group of people, people, words, talking, conversation
  scenery, plural

  You'd have to poke your head out of the alley to get a good look at the
  men. You only know they're there because you can hear them talking.

  on the player listening
    refuse alley-strain-to-hear
  end on

  on every turn while the voices is here and one chance in 4
    phrase alley-still-talking
  end on

define phrase alley-strain-to-hear
  You strain to hear what the men are saying, but can't make out the words.
  Maybe if you got some elevation, you could hear better.
end phrase

## The source draws each word of this from a `[one of]` list — men or
## voices; still talking, continue talking, or continue their conversation;
## outside, just outside, or at the entrance to — inside four sentence
## shapes. Chord randomises whole arms, so each shape carries one draw.

define phrase alley-still-talking, randomly
  The men are still talking outside the alley.
or
  The voices just outside the alley continue their conversation.
or
  You can still hear the men at the entrance to the alley talking.
or
  You can still hear the voices talking just outside the alley.
end phrase

## What the alley's southeast says while they are there (`story.ni:1577`).

define phrase alley-stay-hidden, stopping
  Those men are still talking right outside the alley. Something — an
  intuition? — tells you it would be a good idea to stay out of sight for
  the moment.
or
  Those men are still talking right outside the alley. Better stay out of
  sight until they leave.
end phrase

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE ROOF — the storehouse roof (`story.ni:1584`), reached up the crates
##
## Every other way off it is the source's one refusal; what can be seen from
## it is in `peering.chord` (the junction to the southeast, Lord's Road
## north, the alley below). The briefing spools from HERE, not from the
## group: a phrase whose owner leaves the stage in the same arm never
## renders, and the fifth line is the one that sends the group away. The
## room is always present, so its text always lands.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the Outer Market Roof
  a room
  aka roof, rooftop, market roof, roof top, outer market
  down to the Alley
  north is blocked: roof-precarious
  northeast is blocked: roof-precarious
  east is blocked: roof-precarious
  southeast is blocked: roof-precarious
  south is blocked: roof-precarious
  southwest is blocked: roof-precarious
  west is blocked: roof-precarious
  northwest is blocked: roof-precarious
  up is blocked: roof-precarious

  You're perched atop the red tile roof of the storehouse. From here you have
  a good view of the market's northwest junction and Lord's Road leading out
  of town to the north.

  after the player entering
    phrase roof-hop-up
  end after

  on every turn while the group of mercenaries is here
    first time
      phrase overheard-spread-out
    second time
      phrase overheard-tempers
    third time
      phrase overheard-a-kid
    fourth time
      phrase overheard-parchment
    fifth time
      phrase overheard-go
      move the voices offstage
      move the mercenary captain offstage
      move the group of mercenaries offstage
      change the story to hunted
  end on

create the tiles
  aka red tiles, tile, shingle, shingles, storehouse roof
  scenery, plural
  in the Outer Market Roof

  The roof tiles are warm under the morning sun.

create the view
  aka view of town, vista, landscape, city, town, Miradania, Toresal
  scenery
  in the Outer Market Roof

  You have a good view of the market's northwest junction and Lord's Road
  leading out of town to the north.

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE GROUP OF MERCENARIES — seen from the roof, gathered at the junction
##
## Off-stage until the bite; on the Outer Market Roof for the briefing, as
## the source places them (`story.ni:1604`); off-stage again with the voices
## when the fifth line has been heard. Bare `listen` never reaches an entity
## clause (no target), so the source's roof and alley `Instead of listening`
## lines answer `listen to the mercenaries` / `listen to the voices` only.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the group of mercenaries
  aka mercenaries, mercenary, men, soldiers, people, person, bravos, guards, mercs, group
  scenery, plural

  They're not Lord's Guards, that's for sure. They wear no insignia, for one
  thing. Their arms and armor are mismatched, ugly, and scarred — not an
  unfit description of the men themselves, either. Also, while the Guard
  would be concerned with keeping the peace in Grubber's Market, these bravos
  look ready to clobber anyone who gets in their way, and maybe burn down a
  few stalls while they're at it.

  after the player examining, once
    phrase roof-mercenaries-wonder
  end after

  on the player listening
    phrase roof-listen-carefully
  end on

create the mercenary captain
  aka captain, leader, mercenary leader, older mercenary, grizzled mercenary
  scenery

  The leader of the mercenaries looks a bit older and a bit more grizzled
  than the rest. He also looks smarter. You'll have a hard time fooling him,
  if it comes to that.

define phrase roof-mercenaries-wonder
  So what are they doing here, you wonder? And come to think of it, where are
  the Lord's Guards, anyway?
end phrase

define phrase roof-listen-carefully
  You listen carefully to what the mercenaries are saying...
end phrase

## The briefing — the source's `Table of overheard mercenary utterances`
## (`story.ni:1795-1804`), one row per turn on the roof.

define phrase overheard-spread-out
  '...we know he skulks Grubber's most mornings, so spread out quick, cover
  all streets from the square,' growls one of them, evidently the group's
  leader.
end phrase

define phrase overheard-tempers
  The leader spits on the dusty ground. 'Keep your eyes open and don't take
  guff. But for the love of Brigid, keep your tempers and your swords
  sheathed unless you really need 'em. Ain't bein' paid to toss the locals.'
end phrase

define phrase overheard-a-kid
  'And keep sharp!' the leader snaps. 'Remember this is a kid we're lookin'
  for. Kids move fast, stay out of sight, slip through crowds easy. I find
  out later he got away under your nose, I'll have it off!'

  Suddenly, that bit of apple you swallowed a minute ago turns into a cold,
  hard chunk in your belly.
end phrase

define phrase overheard-parchment
  'Now, everyone memorize the description.' The leader pulls a dirty, folded
  bit of parchment from his belt and squints at it. 'Shoulder-length brown
  hair,' he reads, haltingly. 'Green eyes. Slight of build. Ten, maybe twelve
  spans high.' He holds his hand out flat, at what happens to be exactly your
  height. 'Usually wears a hat and a gray cloak.'

  He refolds the parchment and jams it back into his belt. 'You dims got all
  that?'

  The others nod.
end phrase

define phrase overheard-go
  'Okay, go!' barks the leader, and the mercenaries set out in several
  directions, moving at a brisk walk. Two men go to the north gates and start
  shoving them closed; the others enter the crowded market and start shoving
  their way through the suddenly flabbergasted crowds.

  Your heart sinks. This is *definitely* not good.
end phrase

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE WAY DOWN — the sweep's opening (`story.ni:1725`)
##
## Fired from the Alley's own `after the player entering while hunted, once`
## (`grubbers-market.chord`): the first time Jack sets foot in the alley
## after the briefing is the descent, and the tumble brings the pair in —
## `approaching`, lunge running, and `waiting` reset, since that clock only
## ever runs while the pair is off-stage. The pair's own `when the player
## moves` (`mercenaries.chord`) fires after this clause on the same move and
## is guarded for it: a move that ends with the pair already in her new room
## is an arrival, not an escape.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define phrase roof-hop-up
  You nimbly hop up on top of the crates; from there, it's only a short
  scramble to the roof of the market.
end phrase

define phrase roof-tumble
  As you climb back down the crates, one of them wobbles and your foot slips.
  You tumble loudly to the cobblestones. 'Stupid! Clumsy!' you mutter,
  scrambling to your feet.

  From the street, you hear, 'What was that? Check the alley!'

  Two mercenaries rush in and spot you immediately. They approach warily,
  their arms spread wide.

  You're in trouble now...
end phrase

define phrase alley-ankle-grab
  You try to scramble up the crates, but one of the mercenaries grabs your
  ankle and drags you back down!
end phrase

define phrase roof-precarious
  The rooftop is steep and slick, and the tiles have been known to come loose
  and fall off. It's precarious enough just staying here in one spot. If
  you're to make your escape, you'll have to make it down on the ground.
end phrase
